---
description: Threading and redraw rules for the FTXUI event loop and Jira worker thread
globs: src/**/*.cpp
alwaysApply: false
---

# Never block or bypass the UI thread

`JiraClient` owns one curl handle and is not thread-safe. Every call goes through
`ui::async`, which runs on the single worker thread.

```cpp
// ❌ BAD: freezes the UI for the length of the request, and races the worker
issues_ = ctx_.jira().search(jql);

// ✅ GOOD
ui::async(
    ctx_, life, "Load issues in " + project_.key,
    [this, jql] { return ctx_.jira().search(jql); },
    [this](IssuePage page) { issues_ = std::move(page.issues); });
```

The third argument names the action for the user — "Move ENG-12 to Done", not
"transition". It is the heading of the popup shown when the request fails, so
phrase it from their point of view, not the API's.

Pass `life` (the `Window` member) and let it convert to `weak_ptr`. Capturing the
`shared_ptr` in a callback keeps the token alive after the window dies, so the
expiry check stops working and the callback writes to freed memory.

Need to reset state when a request fails? Use the five-argument form; `done` is
skipped on error.

```cpp
ui::async(ctx_, life, action, work, done, [this] { loading_ = false; });
```

# Posted closures do not redraw

FTXUI invalidates the frame for input events only. `App::post` therefore posts
`Event::Custom` after each closure. Without it, async results update state that
never reaches the screen and the view appears stuck on "loading…". Do not remove
that line, and do not treat `Event::Custom` as a user keypress.
